# The two records that join a finished scan to the keeper work that asked for
# it, and nothing else.
#
# A conductor writes both before it starts a scan and a reporter reads them
# back when the scan ends. Neither is TomoScan's own. The base tomoScan
# template declares no such record at any station, and TomoScan has no idea
# this system exists.
#
# ## This file is the artefact for all three ways of getting them onto a
# ## beamline, and only the loader changes
#
#   softIoc -S -m P=..,R=.. -d records.db    a service of our own, touching
#                                            no beamline file and needing no
#                                            restart of anything
#
#   dbLoadRecords in the station's st.cmd    the same records inside the
#                                            TomoScan IOC, one lifecycle
#                                            instead of two, one restart
#
#   merged into tomoScan.template upstream   every station has them, and the
#                                            Python class can write them into
#                                            the HDF5
#
# The first is what runs today, because a beamline with users on it cannot be
# restarted. The last is the end state, because only there do the ids reach
# the data file and make it self-describing. Nothing about the records
# changes between them, which is why there is one file rather than three.
#
# The macro split is $(P)$(R) rather than one prefix because that is what the
# TomoScan templates use, so the middle route is a dbLoadRecords line and not
# a rewrite. At 2-BM that is P=2bmb: and R=TomoScan:.
#
# ## Two things about the shape
#
# stringout rather than a char waveform. A UUID is 36 characters and
# stringout holds 40. TomoScan serves its own strings as char waveforms,
# which is why both adapters ask for a string rather than assuming one; it is
# not a reason to copy the shape here.
#
# No PINI and no autosave, which is deliberately the opposite of what ScanUUID
# does. A value that survived a restart would be a real, well formed id
# belonging to work this system has lost track of, and a reporter reading it
# would attribute a scan to the wrong step. Empty is the honest value after a
# restart, and both adapters already treat a scan carrying no ids as one
# somebody ran by hand.

record(stringout, "$(P)$(R)KeeperExecutionId") {
    field(DESC, "Keeper execution a scan serves")
}

record(stringout, "$(P)$(R)KeeperStepId") {
    field(DESC, "Keeper step a scan serves")
}
